.TH PEEK 1 "2026-08-01" "1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
peek \- paste an image, video or audio path and see or hear it, or run a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B peek
.PP
.B peek --paste
.I TEXT
.PP
.B peek --view
.I PATH
.RB [ -s
.IR SLOT ]
.RB [ -n
.IR TOTAL ]
.PP
.B peek --audio
.IR PATH ...
.RB [ -s
.IR SLOT ]
.RB [ -n
.IR TOTAL ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A bare rectangle in the middle of the screen with a
.B >
and a field. It exists because a path printed in a terminal is not a picture:
the fastest way to look at something is to paste where it lives and have it
appear.
.PP
The prompt wears
.B no border
\[em] it is a plain dark slab, square-cornered. The picture and video windows
do keep a thin cyan frame, which is what separates them from the desktop
behind.
.PP
.B Audio is the one thing that does not get a window each.
Two pictures can share a screen; two songs cannot share a room. Every audio
file pasted at once lands in a
.B single player
\[em] a list of the file names with a cursor, and one transport bar under it.
Move the cursor with the arrows or
.BR j / k ,
press
.B space
to play what is selected, space again to pause. A lone audio file starts
playing by itself; a list waits, because a menu that picks for you is not a
menu.
.PP
There is no Enter. The field resolves on every pause in typing, so a pasted path
shows itself: an image opens as a floating window, a video opens playing and
looping, both resizable like any floating window. Paste several paths at once
and each one opens as its own window, laid out on a grid sized to the workspace
so none covers another and none falls off the screen. Every window is sized to
its own aspect ratio inside its cell and centred there, so a portrait phone
video is not mostly black bars.
.PP
.B Escape closes every peek window at once
\[em] the prompt, the pictures and the videos \[em] not just the one in front of
you.
.PP
Enter runs whatever else was typed, which is what the second placeholder
promises. The prompt alternates between
.RI \[lq] "pega la ruta de una imagen o un video" \[rq]
and
.RI \[lq] "corre cualquier comando" \[rq],
fading between them, and both are true.
.PP
.B One prompt at a time.
The keybinding takes a lock before it loads anything heavy, so pressing
.B $mod+i
again while a prompt is already coming up costs a few milliseconds and dies
quietly instead of opening a second rectangle.
.SH THE OTHER DIRECTION
Paste a picture instead of a path and the prompt runs in reverse: the image is
written to
.IR /tmp/peek-<timestamp>.png ,
opened in its own window, and its location is left both in the field and on the
clipboard \[em] ready to hand to whoever asked for it.
.PP
This closes the loop with the screenshot binding in its clipboard-only mode:
capture an area, press
.BR $mod+i ,
paste, and the shot has a path.
.SH KEYBINDINGS
.TP
.B $mod+i
Open the prompt. Pressing it again while one is open does nothing.
.TP
.B Escape
Close every peek window.
.TP
.B Ctrl+V
Paste. A picture on the clipboard is saved to
.I /tmp
and its path returned; anything else pastes as text.
.TP
.B Enter
Run the typed text as a command, unless it is already a media path.
.TP
.B "space " "(player)"
Play what the cursor is on; press again to pause it.
.TP
.BR "Up / Down" ", " "j / k" " (player)"
Move the cursor. Home and End jump to the ends. Moving the cursor never starts
or stops anything on its own.
.TP
.B "Enter " "(player)"
Play the selected file from the start, whatever was playing before.
.TP
.B $mod + right-drag
Resize a window, as with any floating window in sway.
.SH USAGE
.TP
.B peek
Open the prompt. This is what the keybinding does.
.TP
.BI "peek --view " PATH
Show one image or play one video directly, with no prompt. Bypasses the
single-prompt lock, so any number of these can be open at once.
.TP
.BI "peek --view " "PATH " "-s " I " -n " N
Show one file as slot
.I I
of
.I N
on the grid. The prompt spawns one of these per path when several are pasted;
each window places itself, so nothing has to coordinate them.
.TP
.BI "peek --audio " "PATH ..." " [-s " I "] [-n " N "]"
Open one player holding every listed audio file, in slot
.I I
of the grid. The prompt spawns exactly one of these however many audio files
were pasted.
.TP
.BI "peek --paste " TEXT
Open the prompt with the field prefilled. This is the testable seam: it exercises
the whole resolve-and-grow path without synthesising a single keystroke.
.TP
.B PEEK_DEBUG=1 peek
Print what the field resolved to, on every pause, to standard error.
.SH WHAT COUNTS AS A PATH
Plain paths, paths with
.BR ~ ,
.I file://
URIs, quoted paths, and several of any of those at once separated by spaces or
newlines. Trailing sentence punctuation is trimmed, so a path pasted out of
prose \[em] ending in a full stop or a comma \[em] still resolves.
.PP
A path is accepted only when the file exists
.B and
its extension is one of:
.TP
.B images
png, jpg, jpeg, webp, gif, bmp, svg, avif, tif, tiff, ico
.TP
.B video
mp4, webm, mkv, mov, avi, m4v, ogv, mpg, mpeg, wmv, flv, ts, m2ts, 3gp
.TP
.B audio
mp3, m4a, aac, flac, wav, ogg, oga, opus, wma, aiff, aif, alac, mka, ape
.IP
.I .ogg
counts as audio, not video: in the wild it is nearly always Vorbis. Theora
video keeps
.IR .ogv .
.PP
Anything else is left alone for Enter to run as a command.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.scripts/peek
The whole program: Python 3 and GTK 4, one file, no build step.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/sway/modes/default
Holds the
.B $mod+i
binding. The binding sets
.B LD_PRELOAD
itself \[em] see
.BR GOTCHAS .
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/sway/config.d/98-application-defaults.conf
Holds the window rule (floating, no border) and the
.B layer_effects "peek"
block that squares the prompt's corners. It deliberately does
.B not
set
.BR corner_radius " \[em] see " GOTCHAS .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/peek-prompt.lock
Flock held for the life of the prompt. Its only job is to make a second
.B $mod+i
a no-op.
.SH PIPELINE
.nf
$mod+i  (LD_PRELOAD=libgtk4-layer-shell.so)
  \[->] peek                       take the lock, or exit
      \[->] prompt, centred, 720 wide, height from the font
          \[->] every pause in typing  parse the field
              \[->] pictures/videos    spawn peek --view per path,
                                       each placing itself on the grid
                  \[->] image          Gtk.Picture
                  \[->] video          Gtk.Video, autoplay + loop
              \[->] every audio path   ONE peek --audio for all of them
                                       list + cursor + transport,
                                       driven by GStreamer playbin
              \[->] anything else      Enter runs it, Escape closes everything
.fi
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B python-gobject
and
.B gtk4
(the program),
.B gtk4-layer-shell
(the prompt's square-cornered surface),
.B swayfx
for
.BR swaymsg ,
which is what positions and closes the windows, and
.BR gstreamer ", " gst-plugins-good ", " gst-plugins-bad " and " gst-libav
for video and audio. Video goes through GTK's own
.B GtkGstMediaFileBuiltin
backend; audio does
.B not
\[em] see
.BR GOTCHAS .
.SH GOTCHAS
.TP
.B "A square CSS border inside a rounded window loses all four corners"
These are ordinary windows, so SwayFX clips the surface to the global
.BR "corner_radius 15" .
A 1px border drawn at the very edge of that surface has its corners eaten by
the clip and reads as four disconnected lines \[em] which is exactly how the
operator described it. The border must carry the same
.B border-radius: 15px
so it follows the window. It cannot be fixed the other way round:
.B corner_radius
in a
.B for_window
rule is a GLOBAL setting, so squaring these windows squares every window on
the machine.
.IP
.B And a rectangular child paints straight over that arc.
A
.B Gtk.Picture
or
.B Gtk.Video
fills its parent's box and erases the border curve, so the frame needs
.B set_overflow(Gtk.Overflow.HIDDEN)
to clip children to its own rounded shape. The audio player hid the problem:
labels never reach the corners, so only the media windows showed it.
.TP
.B "Gtk.MediaFile kills the whole process on an mp3 \[em] audio must bypass GTK"
.B GtkMediaFile
drives
.BR GstPlay ,
which forces
.B playbin3
and
.BR decodebin3 ,
and on this machine that combination aborts:
.IP
.nf
gstdecodebin3.c:3381: mq_slot_handle_stream_start:
assertion failed: (collection)   ->   Bail out!
.fi
.IP
It is a GLib abort, so there is nothing to catch \[em] peek would simply die
mid-paste, taking any other window in the same process with it. Plain
.B playbin
decodes the same file without complaint, so audio is driven straight from
GStreamer and never touches
.BR Gtk.MediaFile .
Verified again on gstreamer 1.28.5, 2026-08-01. Video is unaffected and still
uses
.BR Gtk.Video .
.IP
Import
.B Gst
lazily, inside the audio path only: the prompt must not pay for a GStreamer
init it will never use.
.TP
.B "The theme owns `progress` too \[em] that is three widget nodes now"
Every selector tried \[em]
.BR "progress" ", " "> trough > progress" ", " "background-image: none"
\[em] rendered the fill at (153, 209, 219), which is Catppuccin Frappe
.IR sky ,
and
.B min-height
was ignored outright. Same story as
.B .frame
and the
.B entry
box. The transport bar is two cairo rectangles in a
.B Gtk.DrawingArea
instead, which answers to nobody. The rule: when a GTK widget's internal node
resists app CSS for the second time, stop writing selectors and draw it.
.TP
.B Gtk.Application costs about 400ms before your first line of UI code runs
Measured, interleaved A/B: registering a
.B Gtk.Application
put the prompt on screen in ~857ms; the same program on a bare
.BR Gtk.init "\ +\ " GLib.MainLoop
does it in ~386ms, and every millisecond of that is between the keypress and
anything appearing. Without a GApplication the Wayland
.I app_id
comes from the program name, so
.B GLib.set_prgname("dev.milo.peek")
is load-bearing: the window rule and
.RB \[lq] "[app_id=...] kill" \[rq]
both hang off it.
.TP
.B .frame is a class the GTK theme already owns
Styling
.B .frame
loses the cascade to the theme, silently: the border rendered a dull grey
instead of the intended cyan and nothing anywhere reported a problem. Renaming
the identical rule to
.B .peek-frame
fixed it. Prefix every class in an app stylesheet.
.TP
.B GTK ignores padding, min-height and colour set on the entry node
.BR entry.path ", " .path " and plain " entry
all parse without error and all do nothing;
only the inner
.B text
node answers. So text properties are addressed to
.RB \[lq] ".path text" \[rq]
and the vertical rhythm lives on the row that
.I contains
the entry, not the entry.
.TP
.B Pinning the prompt's height squeezes the text against the top edge
A vertical
.B Gtk.Box
packs its children at the top; forcing the box to 56px while its content
measured 24px left the text glued under the top edge with 32px of dead space
below it. The height now comes from padding, so the row is centred by
construction.
.TP
.B "\[lq]The top of the text is cut off\[rq] was never clipping \[em] it is GTK's AUTOMATIC font rendering"
GTK 4.16 defaults
.B gtk-font-rendering
to
.BR AUTOMATIC ,
and in that mode it rasterises softly: stems land between pixel columns at half
intensity and the flat top of every digit rounds away. It reads as the tops
being sliced off. Nothing is clipped \[em] the glyphs are drawn that way.
.B peek
sets
.B MANUAL
with
.B hintfull
on its own
.BR Gtk.Settings ,
which reproduces the PangoCairo rendering exactly. Per process; nothing else on
the machine changes.
.IP
.B A Gtk.Label is the control that tells you whether your widget is clipping.
A label has no height constraint of any kind, so if it renders a string the
same way your entry does, nothing is being cut. Running label, plain entry,
entry with
.BR min-height ,
entry with
.BR padding-top ,
and a bare
.B Gtk.Text
side by side produced five byte-identical renders \[em] which killed the
clipping theory outright.
.IP
.B Compare against an independent renderer, never against your own render.
Two rounds were lost to a screenshot that matched the operator's pixel for
pixel; both had gone through the same widget. Ground truth means drawing the
string straight through
.B PangoCairo
with no widget involved.
.IP
.B Use digits as the test string.
Round and irregular letters hide a flattened top; digits share one flat cap
height, so the defect reads as a chop across the whole line. The operator
worked this out and typed
.I 11112222233333344444
\[em] that screenshot is what finally made it visible.
.IP
.B And measure the fix the same way you measured the bug.
A
.B min-height
on the text node was shipped as the fix on the strength of a
before/after built with mismatched thresholds. An A/B of the two builds in one
session showed them byte-identical. It was reverted.
.TP
.B Take the single-instance lock before importing gi
The point of the lock is that the second keypress is cheap. Claimed before
.B import gi
it costs ~60ms and exits; claimed after, it would pay the entire GTK startup
first.
.TP
.B corner_radius in a for_window rule is GLOBAL, not per-window
SwayFX documents it as \[lq]set corner radius for new windows\[rq]: it is a
setting, not a window property. Putting it in a
.B for_window
rule does not scope it \[em] it runs the global command every time a matching
window appears, so every window created afterwards came out square. The only
per-surface corner radius SwayFX has is
.B layer_effects
.IR "<namespace>" ,
which applies to layer-shell surfaces, not to ordinary windows.
.TP
.B Never position by [con_id=__focused__]
The window being open is not the window being focused, so a launcher that moves
\[lq]the focused window\[rq] will eventually resize somebody else's. Each peek
window titles itself
.I peek:<pid>
and moves only that title.
.TP
.B move position is relative to the workspace, not the output
The workspace rect already starts below the bar. Adding its origin on top pushed
the bottom row of the grid off the screen \[em] the tell was a window asking for
y=563 and landing at y=603, exactly the bar's 40px.
.TP
.B Lay the grid out on the workspace rect, not the output rect
The output includes the bar; using it makes the last row hang past the bottom.
.TP
.B A video's size is not known until its stream is prepared
.B Gtk.Video
has no intrinsic width at construction. The window is placed on its cell first
and reshaped from
.B notify::prepared
when the real dimensions arrive.
.TP
.B The window having focus is not the entry having it
Without an explicit
.B grab_focus
on the field, keystrokes go nowhere and the prompt looks broken.
.TP
.B GTK cannot fade its built-in placeholder
The alternating hint is a label of its own in a
.BR Gtk.Overlay ,
shown only while the field is empty, with its opacity tweened by hand.
.TP
.B Do not test this by synthesising keystrokes
.B wtype
types into whatever holds focus, and this window takes focus. Typing into it
while the operator is using the keyboard interleaves both streams: the path
arrives corrupted and it looks like the tool dropped characters. Use
.B --paste
instead, which needs no keyboard at all, or the nested headless sway harness.
.TP
.B Escape closes all of them on purpose
It kills by
.IR app_id ,
so one Escape ends the whole session of windows. That is the intent, not a
side effect.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sway (5),
.BR swaymsg (1),
.BR swayimg (1),
.BR projects (1),
